lso passed on first attempt.\\\"\\n      }\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"candidatePatterns\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CPAT-001\\\",\\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"narrow-scope-crossref-with-verified-anchors\\\",\\n      \\\"strength\\\": \\\"medium\\\",\\n      \\\"occurrences\\\": 5,\\n      \\\"tasks\\\": [\\\"T-003\\\", \\\"T-004\\\", \\\"T-006\\\", \\\"T-007\\\", \\\"T-008\\\"],\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Cross-reference update tasks with narrow scope (4-8 acceptance criteria) consistently achieved first-attempt acceptance. Tasks that explicitly verified anchor existence and identifier resolution before submission (T-007 documented this in notes) had zero review issues. Pattern: keep changes minimal, mirror existing structural patterns on the page, verify all cross-reference targets.\\\",\\n      \\\"invariantCombination\\\": [\\\"INV-crossref-002\\\", \\\"INV-crossref-003\\\", \\\"INV-crossref-005\\\", \\\"INV-crossref-009\\\", \\\"INV-style-001\\\"],\\n      \\\"patternsCited\\\": [\\\"PAT-009\\\"],\\n      \\\"recommendationsCited\\\": [\\\"REC-005\\\"]\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CPAT-002\\\",\\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"mirror-existing-page-structure\\\",\\n      \\\"strength\\\": \\\"low\\\",\\n      \\\"occurrences\\\": 2,\\n      \\\"tasks\\\": [\\\"T-004\\\", \\\"T-008\\\"],\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"When inserting a new callout or note, mirroring the exact structure and phrasing of an existing callout on the same page achieved first-attempt acceptance. T-004 explicitly noted it 'matching the exact structural pattern of the existing Create a new page permission note.' This approach reduces reviewer friction because the style is pre-validated by the existing content.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"candidateAntiPatterns\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CAPAT-001\\\",\\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"must-directed-at-people-in-permission-docs\\\",\\n      \\\"strength\\\": \\\"high\\\",\\n      \\\"occurrences\\\": 3,\\n      \\\"tasks\\\": [\\\"T-001\\\", \\\"T-002\\\", \\\"T-005\\\"],\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"INV-style-132 ('must' directed at people) was the sole or primary rejection cause in all 3 multi-cycle tasks. The writer consistently produced phrases like 'you must have', 'the user must have', 'they must advance' when documenting permission requirements -- a context where imperative phrasing feels natural but violates the style guide. All 3 fixes were mechanical: 'must' -> 'need to' / 'needs to'. This is a systematic writer tendency specifically triggered by permission-requirement content.\\\",\\n      \\\"rootCause\\\": \\\"style\\\",\\n      \\\"invariant\\\": \\\"INV-style-132\\\",\\n      \\\"fix\\\": \\\"Replace 'must' with 'need to' / 'needs to' when the subject is a person. Alternative: rephrase so the requirement is the subject ('a role assignment is required').\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CAPAT-002\\\",\\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"multi-sentence-callout-missing-bold-title\\\",\\n      \\\"strength\\\": \\\"medium\\\",\\n      \\\"occurrences\\\": 2,\\n      \\\"tasks\\\": [\\\"T-001\\\", \\\"T-002\\\"],\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"INV-style-139 requires multi-sentence callouts to have a bold title on a separate line. Both T-001 (warning callout) and T-002 (3 note callouts) omitted this title. The writer correctly exempted single-sentence callouts in T-002, showing awareness of the rule but incomplete application when writing multi-sentence content. Both fixes were structural: add bold title line after the opening Liquid callout tag.\\\",\\n      \\\"rootCause\\\": \\\"style\\\",\\n      \\\"invariant\\\": \\\"INV-style-139\\\",\\n      \\\"fix\\\": \\\"Always add a bold title on a separate line after callout opening Liquid tag when the callout body has 2+ sentences.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"styleEvolutions\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"observation\\\": \\\"Persona reviewer surfaced 'inherit' as too technical for business users in T-005; writer incorporated rephrasing alongside style fix\\\",\\n      \\\"occurrences\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"strength\\\": \\\"low\\\",\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"The persona reviewer flagged 'does not inherit permissions from its parent' as using system-administration vocabulary inappropriate for business users, suggesting 'uses its own permission settings instead of the parent page's'. This was a non-blocking note (approved-with-notes) but the writer adopted it during the style fix rewrite. Only 1 occurrence -- not enough to establish a pattern, but signals that permission-domain vocabulary may need a business-user glossary.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"observation\\\": \\\"Style reviewer issued 'approved-with-notes' for pre-existing checkbox spelling issue in T-003 (workflows.md)\\\",\\n      \\\"occurrences\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"strength\\\": \\\"low\\\",\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"During GAP-002 re-entry review of T-003, the style reviewer noted a pre-existing issue (checkbox spelling) not introduced by the pipeline changes. This did not block approval but signals that the style reviewer catches issues beyond the immediate diff scope. Not actionable as a style evolution -- the pre-existing issue predates this pipeline run.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"gapResolutionAnalysis\\\": {\\n    \\\"gapsTotal\\\": 3,\\n    \\\"gapsResolved\\\": 3,\\n    \\\"gapDetails\\\": [\\n      {\\n        \\\"gapId\\\": \\\"GAP-001\\\",\\n        \\\"description\\\": \\\"Clone page permission requirements undocumented\\\",\\n        \\\"affectedTasks\\\": [\\\"T-001\\\", \\\"T-004\\\"],\\n        \\\"resolution\\\": \\\"T-001 added clone permission requirements to 'Page permissions for creating pages' section. T-004 added info callout to 'Clone a page' section with cross-reference.\\\",\\n        \\\"reEntryAttempts\\\": 1,\\n        \\\"reEntryVerdict\\\": \\\"approved\\\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \\\"gapId\\\": \\\"GAP-002\\\",\\n        \\\"description\\\": \\\"Unicode en dashes (U+2013