      "finish_reason": "tool_calls",
      "logprobs": null,
      "message": {
        "reasoning_text": "Good, I now have a complete picture. Let me now write all the files. I'll create a script to do all the writing at once since there are many files.\n\nLet me plan my entries one more time:\n\n**Updates (3):**\n1. PAT-009 → promote to `high` (3rd run, 80% acceptance rate for cross-ref tasks). Update usageCount to 3, add DOC-3167 evidence.\n2. STYLE-002 → add DOC-3167 evidence, keep `medium`, update usageCount to 3.\n3. ANTI-004 → add DOC-3167 evidence, keep `medium`, update usageCount to 3. Add note about briefing not preventing violation.\n\n\n4. PAT-012 → new entry for mirroring existing page structure with callout/note insertions at `low` priority.\n5. ANTI-012 → new entry capturing how permission-requirement prose systematically triggers 'must' language directed at people, also `low`.\n6. DOM-021 → new entry about multi-layer permission models needing per-layer documentation sections with explicit interaction rules at `low`.\n7. DOM-022 → new entry for pipeline-modified files that may contain pre-existing encoding violations undetectable without tooling at `low`.\n8. DOM-023 → new entry for operations with non-standard permission checking patterns requiring dedicated documentation callouts at `low`.\n9. DOM-024 → new entry about workflow step role checks that may be asymmetrical, also `low` priority.\n10. SRC-OBS-005 → new entry predicting that permission operations with unique permission combinations will need mandatory documentation coverage at `low`.\n11. SRC-OBS-006 → new entry predicting that recursive permission check parameters will need documentation callouts for scope-of-check at `low`.\n12. SRC-OBS-007 → new entry predicting that hub pages absorbing over 40% of total impacts will need critical-risk tasks with 2+ review cycles at `low`.\n\nI'm also discarding three entries: DI-6 because the impact mapper already handles that cross-cutting correlation, SE-1 because it's a single occurrence that DOM-008 already covers, and SE-2 because it was self-identified as not actionable.\n\nNow I'm organizing the full document with updated entries first, then new ones, followed by retrospective analysis, index, research sources, status summary, and manifest.",
        "role": "assistant",
        "reasoning_opaque": "",
        "content": "Now I have complete understanding of all inputs. Let me execute the integration.\n\n**Deduplication analysis summary:**\n- CPAT-001 (narrow-scope-crossref) → `confirm-existing` PAT-009 (promote to `high`)\n- CPAT-002 (mirror existing structure) → `new` PAT-012\n- CAPAT-001 (must in permission prose) → `new` ANTI-012\n- CAPAT-002 (multi-sentence callout title) → `confirm-existing` STYLE-002\n- GAP-001/GAP-003 signals → `confirm-existing` ANTI-004\n- 4 domain insights, 3 source observations → `new`\n- 3 signals discarded (quality gate failures)\n\nLet me write all the files:",
        "refusal": null,
        "tool_calls": [
          {
            "id": "toolu_vrtx_01DuAspWu3wVxnoSaScBbHJL",
            "type": "function",
            "function": {
              "name": "report_intent",
              "arguments": "{\"intent\": \"Writing knowledge entries\"}"
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "toolu_vrtx_0126KaBkYWTjvEH8pFdmCgmn",
            "type": "function",
            "function": {
              "name": "edit",
              "arguments": "{\"path\": \"/workspace/.docwriter/meta/patterns/PAT-009.md\", \"old_str\": \"---\\nid: PAT-009\\ntitle: \\\"Cross-reference update tasks achieve near-perfect first-attempt rates\\\"\\ntype: pattern\\nconfidence: medium\\ndomains: [\\\"cross-references\\\", \\\"pipeline-process\\\"]\\ndiscoveredDate: \\\"2026-03-15T06:23:39Z\\\"\\nlastReferencedDate: \\\"2026-03-15T18:03:22Z\\\"\\nusageCount: 2\\nsourceTasks: [\\\"T-013\\\", \\\"T-014\\\", \\\"T-015\\\", \\\"T-016\\\", \\\"DOC-2847-T-005\\\"]\\ninvariantsReferenced: [\\\"INV-structure-017\\\"]\\nsourceSignals: [\\\"SIG-007\\\"]\\ndeprecated: false\\n---\\n\\n## Insight\\n\\nCross-reference update tasks have significantly higher first-attempt acceptance rates than content creation or content update tasks. 3 of 4 cross-ref update tasks (T-013, T-014, T-015) achieved first-attempt success. The sole failure (T-016) was a single em dash — content and cross-refs were correct on attempt 1.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\n- 75% first-attempt rate for cross-ref updates vs 56% overall and 0% for create tasks\\n- Cross-ref updates with INV-structure-017 applied have 100% content-accuracy first-attempt rate\\n- Signal strength: medium (4 tasks within a single run)\\n- Confidence: low (first observation in a single run)\\n\\n## Recommendation\\n\\nCross-ref update tasks can be deprioritized for pre-writing review overhead and are well-suited for parallel execution batches. Their predictable success rate reduces risk allocation needs.\\n\\n### Run 3 (DOC-2847)\\n\\n- Run 3 (DOC-2847): T-005 was a cross-reference insertion task with verified link target identifiers and descriptive link text. First-attempt acceptance. Style reviewer approved-with-notes (all notes positive). Confirms pattern: narrow scope + verified anchors = low risk for cross-ref tasks.\\n- Promoted: low → medium (2nd pipeline run confirmation)\\n\\n## Applicability\\n\\nApplicable to any pipeline run that includes cross-reference update tasks as part of the task graph. Cross-ref tasks can be deprioritized for review cycle budgeting and are well-suited for parallel execution.\", \"new_str\": \"---\\nid: PAT-009\\ntitle: \\\"Cross-reference update tasks achieve near-perfect first-attempt rates\\\"\\ntype: pattern\\nconfidence: high\\ndomains: [\\\"cross-references\\\", \\\"pipeline-process\\\", \\\"content-security\\\"]\\ndiscoveredDate: \\\"2026-03-15T06:23:39Z\\\"\\nlastReferencedDate: \\\"2026-03-18T22:24:04Z\\\"\\nusageCount: 3\\nsourceTasks: [\\\"T-013\\\", \\\"T-014\\\", \\\"T-015\\\", \\\"T-016\\\", \\\"DOC-2847-T-005\\\", \\\"DOC-3167-T-003\\\", \\\"DOC-3167-T-004\\\", \\\"DOC-3167-T-006\\\", \\\"DOC-3167-T-007\\\", \\\"DOC-3167-T-008\\\"]\\ninvariantsReferenced: [\\\"INV-structure-017\\\", \\\"INV-crossref-002\\\", \\\"INV-crossref-003\\\", \\\"INV-crossref-005\\\", \\\"INV-crossref-009\\\"]\\nsourceSignals: [\\\"SIG-007\\\", \\\"CPAT-001\\\"]\\ndeprecated: false\\n---\\n\\n## Insight\\n\\nCross-reference update tasks have significantly higher first-attempt acceptance rates than content creation or content update tasks. Tasks with narrow scope (≤8 acceptance criteria), verified anchor existence, and no heading changes consistently achieve first-attempt acceptance. The sole failures across 3 runs were caused by unrelated style issues (em dash, INV-style-132), not cross-reference problems.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\n### Run 1 (original)\\n\\n- 75% first-attempt rate for cross-ref updates vs 56% overall and 0% for create tasks\\n- Cross-ref updates with INV-structure-017 applied have 100% content-accuracy first-attempt rate\\n- Signal strength: medium (4 tasks within a single run)\\n\\n### Run 2 (DOC-2847)\\n\\n- T-005 was a cross-reference insertion task with verified link target identifiers and descriptive link text. First-attempt acceptance. Style reviewer approved-with-notes (all notes positive). Confirms pattern: narrow scope + verified anchors = low risk.\\n- Promoted: low → medium (2nd pipeline run confirmation)\\n\\n### Run 3 (DOC-3167)\\n\\n- 5 cross-reference tasks (T-003, T-004, T-006, T-007, T-008): 80% first-attempt acceptance (4/5). T-005 was the only failure, caused solely by INV-style-132 ('must' directed at people), not cross-reference issues.\\n- T-007 was cleanest outcome in entire run: single attempt, no re-entry, all reviewers approved. Explicitly verified anchor existence and identifier resolution before submission. Fewest acceptance criteria (4).\\n- PAT-009 was explicitly cited by T-002, T-004, T-005, T-007 (4 tasks). 3 of 4 citing tasks achieved first-attempt acceptance.\\n- Acceptance rate across 3 runs: Run 1 75% (3/4), Run 2 100% (1/1), Run 3 80% (4/5) = 80% aggregate (8/10)\\n- **Promoted: medium → high** (3rd pipeline run confirmation, aggregate acceptance rate 80%)\\n\\n## Recommendation\\n\\nCross-ref update tasks can be deprioritized for pre-writing review overhead and are well-suited for parallel execution batches. Their predictable success rate reduces risk allocation needs. Tasks with ≤8 acceptance criteria and comprehensive cross-reference invariant application (INV-crossref-002/003/005/009) are lowest risk.\\n\\n## Applicability\\n\\nApplicable to any pipeline run that includes cross-reference update tasks as part of the task graph. Cross-ref tasks can be deprioritized for review cycle budgeting and are well-suited for parallel execution.\"}"
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "toolu_vrtx_01Q8enWovAD49dMpDKyQsEnj",
            "type": "function",
            "function": {
              "name": "edit",
              "arguments": "{\"path\": \"/workspace/.docwriter/meta/style-evolutions/STYLE-002.md\", \"old_str\": \"---\\nid: STYLE-002\\ntitle: \\\"Callout bold title requirement (INV-callout-002) triggered rejections in 3 tasks\\\"\\ntype: style-evolution\\nconfidence: medium\\ndomains: [\\\"style-formatting\\\", \\\"invariant-system\\\"]\\ndiscoveredDate: \\\"2026-03-14T16:06:35+00:00\\\"\\nlastReferencedDate: \\\"2026-03-15T06:23:39Z\\\"\\nusageCount: 2\\nsourceTasks: []\\ninvariantsReferenced: [\\\"INV-callout-002\\\"]\\nsourceSignals: [\\\"SE-002\\\", \\\"SIG-009\\\", \\\"STYLE-EVO-002\\\"]\\ndeprecated: false\\n---\\n\\n## Insight\\n\\nMulti-sentence callouts must have a bold title on the first line. Writers omit this consistently — suggests the invariant needs more prominent placement in writer briefings.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\n### Run 2 (DOC-3137)\\n\\n- Run 2 (DOC-3137): Callout bold title omission in T-010 and T-011, co-occurring with em dash errors. Confirms both issues share a common root: insufficient post-composition style checking.\\n- Acceptance rate across 2 runs: confirmed\\n- Promoted: low → medium (2nd pipeline run confirmation)\\n\\n### Run 1 (original)\\n\\n- Source signal: STYLE-EVO-002 (from task-signals)\\n- Within-run strength: strong (3+ tasks)\\n- Tasks referenced: N/A\\n- Confidence: low (cold-start run — first observation)\\n\\n## Recommendation\\n\\nSee insight above for actionable guidance.\\n\\n## Applicability\\n\\nApplicable to future pipeline runs involving style-formatting, invariant-system documentation domains. Promoted to medium after confirmation in second pipeline run (DOC-3137).\", \"new_str\": \"---\\nid: STYLE-002\\ntitle: \\\"Callout bold title requirement (INV-callout-002/INV-style-139) triggered rejections across 4 runs\\\"\\ntype: style-evolution\\nconfidence: medium\\ndomains: [\\\"style-formatting\\\", \\\"invariant-system\\\", \\\"content-security\\\"]\\ndiscoveredDate: \\\"2026-03-14T16:06:35+00:00\\\"\\nlastReferencedDate: \\\"2026-03-18T22:24:04Z\\\"\\nusageCount: 3\\nsourceTasks: [\\\"DOC-3167-T-001\\\", \\\"DOC-3167-T-002\\\"]\\ninvariantsReferenced: [\\\"INV-callout-002\\\", \\\"INV-style-139\\\"]\\nsourceSignals: [\\\"SE-002\\\", \\\"SIG-009\\\", \\\"STYLE-EVO-002\\\", \\\"CAPAT-002\\\"]\\ndeprecated: false\\n---\\n\\n## Insight\\n\\nMulti-sentence callouts must have a bold title on the first line. Writers omit this consistently — suggests the invariant needs more prominent placement in writer briefings. This is one of the most persistent style violations across pipeline runs.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\n### Run 4 (DOC-3167)\\n\\n- T-001: Warning callout for Read permission prerequisite omitted bold title. Fix: added `**Read permission prerequisite**` after opening Liquid warning tag.\\n- T-002: 3 of 4 permission note callouts (Publish, Revert, Unpublish) had 2+ sentences without bold titles. Move note (single sentence) was correctly exempted. Fix: added `**Required permissions**` bold title to multi-sentence callouts.\\n- Writer showed partial awareness (correctly exempted single-sentence callout) but failed to apply the rule consistently to multi-sentence callouts.\\n- INV-style-139 was the enforcing invariant in this run (same rule as INV-callout-002 in earlier runs).\\n\\n### Run 2 (DOC-3137)\\n\\n- Callout bold title omission in T-010 and T-011, co-occurring with em dash errors. Confirms both issues share a common root: insufficient post-composition style checking.\\n- Promoted: low → medium (2nd pipeline run confirmation)\\n\\n### Run 1 (original)\\n\\n- Source signal: STYLE-EVO-002 (from task-signals)\\n- Within-run strength: strong (3+ tasks)\\n\\n## Recommendation\\n\\nWriter briefings should include an explicit checklist item: \\\"For every callout with 2+ sentences, verify a bold title exists on the first line after the opening Liquid tag.\\\" Consider automated pre-submission check for callout structure.\\n\\n## Applicability\\n\\nApplicable to all pipeline runs involving callout/note insertions. The violation is most common when writing permission-requirement or prerequisite callouts where the writer focuses on content accuracy and overlooks structural formatting. Not promotable to `high` yet — STYLE-002 was not in the knowledge brief for DOC-3167, so no acceptance-rate-when-applied data available.\"}"
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "toolu_vrtx_01N7YaFMRwK2fGQ46m4Lt8qC",
            "type": "function",
            "function": {
              "name": "edit",
              "arguments": "{\"path\": \"/workspace/.docwriter/meta/anti-patterns/ANTI-004.md\", \"old_str\": \"---\\nid: ANTI-004\\ntitle: \\\"When acceptance criteria say 'document methods of interface X' without enumerati\\\"\\ntype: anti-pattern\\nconfidence: medium\\ndomains: [\\\"api-reference\\\", \\\"xperience-platform\\\", \\\"risk-analysis\\\"]\\ndiscoveredDate: \\\"2026-03-14T16:06:35+00:00\\\"\\nlastReferencedDate: \\\"2026-03-15T06:23:39Z\\\"\\nusageCount: 2\\nsourceTasks: []\\ninvariantsReferenced: []\\nsourceSignals: [\\\"SIG-R2-metaknowledge\\\", \\\"CTXGAP-003\\\"]\\ndeprecated: false\\n---\\n\\n## Insight\\n\\nWhen acceptance criteria say 'document methods of interface X' without enumerating them, writers can miss less-prominent methods. Code analysis identified 6 public methods on IContentItemMemberRoleRetriever but the task planner did not convert this into an exhaustive checklist. High-accuracy-risk tasks should have method-level acceptance criteria.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\n### Run 2 (DOC-3137)\\n\\n- Run 2 (DOC-3137): Task planner enumerated IActivityModifier.Modify() and IActivityLogValidator.IsValid() explicitly. No methods were missed. ANTI-004 mitigation (explicit method enumeration) confirmed effective.\\n- Acceptance rate across 2 runs: confirmed\\n- Promoted: low → medium (2nd pipeline run confirmation)\\n\\n### Run 1 (original)\\n\\n- Source signal: CTXGAP-003 (from context-signals)\\n- Within-run strength: single observation\\n- Tasks referenced: N/A\\n- Confidence: low (cold-start run — first observation)\\n\\n## Recommendation\\n\\nSee insight above for actionable guidance.\\n\\n## Applicability\\n\\nApplicable to future pipeline runs involving api-reference, xperience-platform, risk-analysis documentation domains. Promoted to medium after confirmation in second pipeline run (DOC-3137).\", \"new_str\": \"---\\nid: ANTI-004\\ntitle: \\\"Incomplete operation/method enumeration in acceptance criteria causes coverage gaps\\\"\\ntype: anti-pattern\\nconfidence: medium\\ndomains: [\\\"api-reference\\\", \\\"xperience-platform\\\", \\\"risk-analysis\\\", \\\"content-security\\\"]\\ndiscoveredDate: \\\"2026-03-14T16:06:35+00:00\\\"\\nlastReferencedDate: \\\"2026-03-18T22:24:04Z\\\"\\nusageCount: 3\\nsourceTasks: [\\\"DOC-3167-T-001\\\", \\\"DOC-3167-T-004\\\", \\\"DOC-3167-T-008\\\"]\\ninvariantsReferenced: []\\nsourceSignals: [\\\"SIG-R2-metaknowledge\\\", \\\"CTXGAP-003\\\", \\\"GAP-001-signal\\\", \\\"GAP-003-signal\\\"]\\ndeprecated: false\\n---\\n\\n## Insight\\n\\nWhen acceptance criteria say 'document methods of interface X' or 'document permission requirements for operations' without exhaustively enumerating them, writers miss less-prominent items. Code analysis provides complete inventories, but the task planner may not convert these into exhaustive checklists. High-accuracy-risk tasks should have item-level acceptance criteria cross-checked against code-analysis outputs.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\n### Run 4 (DOC-3167) — **VIOLATED DESPITE BRIEFING**\\n\\n- ANTI-004 was included in the knowledge brief for this run. The brief explicitly warned: \\\"code analysis should feed explicit checklists into task planning.\\\"\\n- Code-analysis permissionSummary contained 18 operations. Task planner scoped only 6.\\n- GAP-001: Clone operation (requires READ on source + CREATE on target parent) was excluded from scope despite being fully documented in code analysis. Risk register noted \\\"out of scope\\\" without flagging the exclusion as a gap risk.\\n- GAP-003: Synchronize operation (requires ancestor-chain ACL checks) was omitted entirely — not listed in scope or out-of-scope sets.\\n- Both gaps were caught by gap hunter in cycle 1 and resolved via re-entry, but cost an additional verification cycle.\\n- **Note**: The anti-pattern warning was available but the task planner did not cross-check scoped operations against the full permissionSummary. Mitigation needs stronger enforcement — passive briefing is insufficient.\\n- Confidence kept at `medium` — cannot promote to `high` because the mitigation was not effectively applied despite being briefed.\\n\\n### Run 2 (DOC-3137)\\n\\n- Task planner enumerated IActivityModifier.Modify() and IActivityLogValidator.IsValid() explicitly. No methods were missed. ANTI-004 mitigation (explicit method enumeration) confirmed effective.\\n- Promoted: low → medium (2nd pipeline run confirmation)\\n\\n### Run 1 (original)\\n\\n- Source signal: CTXGAP-003 (from context-signals)\\n- Within-run strength: single observation\\n\\n## Recommendation\\n\\nTask planner MUST cross-check scoped items against the full code-analysis inventory (permissionSummary, method lists, operation enumerations). Each exclusion must be explicitly justified with a gap-risk assessment. Operations with unique behavioral characteristics (different permission combinations, recursive checks, asymmetric workflow requirements) should never be excluded without documented rationale.\\n\\n## Applicability\\n\\nApplicable to any pipeline run where code-analysis provides an enumerated inventory of items (methods, operations, permissions, events) and the task planner scopes a subset. The anti-pattern is especially dangerous for permission documentation where operations with unique semantics can be silently omitted.\"}"
            }
          }
        ],
        "parsed": null
      }
    }
  ],
