2026-03-18T22:27:04.940Z [DEBUG] response (Request-ID 00000-185dadca-87fe-4927-9504-3aaad56e6d28):
2026-03-18T22:27:04.940Z [DEBUG] data:
2026-03-18T22:27:04.941Z [DEBUG] {
  "usage": {
    "completion_tokens": 3671,
    "prompt_tokens": 68769,
    "prompt_tokens_details": {
      "cached_tokens": 63697
    },
    "total_tokens": 72440
  },
  "id": "msg_vrtx_01Foo7EZP48wZLXyfkac2PSD",
  "choices": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "finish_reason": "tool_calls",
      "logprobs": null,
      "message": {
        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "Now let me create the new entries:",
        "refusal": null,
        "tool_calls": [
          {
            "id": "toolu_vrtx_019ExDB4mzxQAV9GUAi2HAfg",
            "type": "function",
            "function": {
              "name": "create",
              "arguments": "{\"path\": \"/workspace/.docwriter/meta/patterns/PAT-012.md\", \"file_text\": \"---\\nid: PAT-012\\ntitle: \\\"Mirror existing page structure when inserting new callouts or notes\\\"\\ntype: pattern\\nconfidence: low\\ndomains: [\\\"cross-references\\\", \\\"content-security\\\", \\\"pipeline-process\\\"]\\ndiscoveredDate: \\\"2026-03-18T22:24:04Z\\\"\\nlastReferencedDate: \\\"2026-03-18T22:24:04Z\\\"\\nusageCount: 1\\nsourceTask: DOC-3167\\ninvariantsReferenced: [\\\"INV-style-001\\\", \\\"INV-style-132\\\", \\\"INV-persona-013\\\"]\\ndeprecated: false\\n---\\n\\n## Insight\\n\\nWhen inserting a new callout or note into an existing documentation page, mirroring the exact structure and phrasing of an existing callout on the same page achieves first-attempt acceptance. The existing content serves as a pre-validated template — its style, formatting, and structural conventions have already passed review. This reduces reviewer friction because the new content is structurally consistent with its surroundings.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\n- DOC-3167 T-004: Inserted permission note for 'Clone a page' section by matching the exact structural pattern of the existing 'Create a new page' permission note on the same page. First-attempt acceptance.\\n- DOC-3167 T-008: Inserted permission cross-reference note by matching existing note patterns. First-attempt acceptance.\\n- Both tasks had narrow scope (5 acceptance criteria each) and explicitly mirrored existing content structure.\\n- Acceptance rate: 100% (2/2 tasks using this approach)\\n\\n## Applicability\\n\\nApplicable to any update task where new structured content (callouts, notes, info boxes, warning blocks) is inserted into a page that already contains similar elements. Less applicable when creating entirely new pages or when the existing content on the page has known style issues.\\n\"}"
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "toolu_vrtx_01M7xjU6qgiZJvSoAUfgTc7C",
            "type": "function",
            "function": {
              "name": "create",
              "arguments": "{\"path\": \"/workspace/.docwriter/meta/anti-patterns/ANTI-012.md\", \"file_text\": \"---\\nid: ANTI-012\\ntitle: \\\"Permission-requirement prose systematically triggers 'must' directed at people (INV-style-132)\\\"\\ntype: anti-pattern\\nconfidence: low\\ndomains: [\\\"content-security\\\", \\\"style-formatting\\\", \\\"invariant-system\\\"]\\ndiscoveredDate: \\\"2026-03-18T22:24:04Z\\\"\\nlastReferencedDate: \\\"2026-03-18T22:24:04Z\\\"\\nusageCount: 1\\nsourceTask: DOC-3167\\ninvariantsReferenced: [\\\"INV-style-132\\\"]\\ndeprecated: false\\n---\\n\\n## Insight\\n\\nPermission-requirement documentation contexts systematically trigger INV-style-132 violations ('must' directed at people). The writer consistently produces phrases like 'you must have', 'the user must have', 'they must advance' when documenting what permissions are required for an operation — a context where imperative phrasing feels natural but violates the style guide. This is not a general 'must' problem; it is specifically triggered by the permission-requirement content domain where requirements are inherently directed at people.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\n- DOC-3167 T-001: Used 'must have' and 'must advance' in permission requirement sentences. Rejected by style reviewer. Fix: 'must have' → 'needs to have', 'must advance' → 'need to advance'.\\n- DOC-3167 T-002: Used 'You must also be able to' in Revert permission note. Rejected by style reviewer. Fix: → 'You also need to be able to'.\\n- DOC-3167 T-005: Used 'you must have the Delete permission'. Rejected by style reviewer. Fix: → 'you need to have'. Third instance despite INV-style-132 being listed in applied invariants.\\n- All 3 multi-cycle outcomes in the run were caused (solely or primarily) by INV-style-132. All 3 fixes were mechanical: 'must' → 'need to' / 'needs to'.\\n- 3 of 8 tasks (37.5%) failed for this reason. 100% of multi-cycle outcomes had this as a root cause.\\n\\n## Recommendation\\n\\nFor permission-documentation tasks, the content writer should:\\n1. Pre-emptively search the draft for 'must' after composition and replace with 'need to' / 'needs to' when the subject is a person.\\n2. Alternative: rephrase so the requirement is the subject ('a role assignment is required' instead of 'you must have a role assignment').\\n3. Consider adding a task-scoped invariant (TINV) specifically for permission tasks: \\\"Replace all instances of 'must' + person-subject with 'need to' before submission.\\\"\\n\\n## Applicability\\n\\nApplicable to any pipeline run involving permission, authorization, or access-control documentation where requirements are expressed as things users/administrators need to have or do. The trigger is the semantic domain (requirements directed at people), not a specific product or technology.\\n\"}"
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "toolu_vrtx_01GYarcjDRud9RWWB5T6FAwJ",
            "type": "function",
            "function": {
              "name": "create",
              "arguments": "{\"path\": \"/workspace/.docwriter/meta/domain-insights/DOM-021.md\", \"file_text\": \"---\\nid: DOM-021\\ntitle: \\\"Multi-layer permission models require per-layer documentation sections with explicit interaction rules\\\"\\ntype: domain-insight\\nconfidence: low\\ndomains: [\\\"content-security\\\", \\\"xperience-platform\\\", \\\"reference\\\"]\\ndiscoveredDate: \\\"2026-03-18T22:24:04Z\\\"\\nlastReferencedDate: \\\"2026-03-18T22:24:04Z\\\"\\nusageCount: 1\\nsourceTask: DOC-3167\\ninvariantsReferenced: []\\ndeprecated: false\\n---\\n\\n## Insight\\n\\nPermission systems with multiple interacting layers (e.g., application-level → ACL → workflow) require per-layer documentation sections with explicit documentation of inter-layer interaction rules. The Xperience page permission model exemplifies this:\\n\\n- **Layer 1 — Application**: Channel admin bypass (skips ACL entirely)\\n- **Layer 2 — ACL**: WebPageAclPermissionEvaluator checks page-level permissions. Read is a prerequisite — granting Update/Create/Delete/Synchronize without Read has no effect (lines 118-123).\\n- **Layer 3 — Workflow**: Workflow step role checks apply to some operations (publish) but not others (unpublish), even when both share the same ACL permission (READ + UPDATE).\\n\\nWithout per-layer sections, the documentation conflates permission requirements from different enforcement points, leading to incomplete or misleading guidance.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\n- DOC-3167 T-001: Required 19 acceptance criteria and 2 initial review cycles to correctly document the 3-layer interaction model. The Read prerequisite rule spans layers (ACL enforcement, affects workflow operations).\\n- DOC-3167 T-003: Workflows page required careful disambiguation of which operations have workflow step checks vs which don't, despite sharing ACL permissions.\\n- The hub page (page-permission-management.md) organized content by operation rather than by layer, requiring cross-cutting callouts to document layer interactions.\\n\\n## Applicability\\n\\nApplicable to any product with 2+ permission enforcement layers that interact (RBAC + attribute-based, ACL + workflow, role-based + resource-based). When code analysis reveals multiple permission evaluation points in the call chain, the task planner should structure documentation to explicitly address each layer and their interactions. Less applicable to simple single-layer permission models.\\n\"}"
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "toolu_vrtx_01NauPHCVRcYNM1jwkzkQLRo",
            "type": "function",
            "function": {
              "name": "create",
              "arguments": "{\"path\": \"/workspace/.docwriter/meta/domain-insights/DOM-022.md\", \"file_text\": \"---\\nid: DOM-022\\ntitle: \\\"Pipeline-modified files may contain pre-existing encoding violations undetectable without tooling\\\"\\ntype: domain-insight\\nconfidence: low\\ndomains: [\\\"style-formatting\\\", \\\"pipeline-process\\\"]\\ndiscoveredDate: \\\"2026-03-18T22:24:04Z\\\"\\nlastReferencedDate: \\\"2026-03-18T22:24:04Z\\\"\\nusageCount: 1\\nsourceTask: DOC-3167\\ninvariantsReferenced: [\\\"INV-style-096\\\"]\\ndeprecated: false\\n---\\n\\n## Insight\\n\\nFiles modified by the pipeline may contain pre-existing encoding violations — Unicode en dashes (U+2013), curly quotes, non-ASCII whitespace — that the content writer cannot reasonably detect without tooling. These violations predate the pipeline run and are invisible in normal editing. When the pipeline modifies these files, the gap hunter detects the violations and attributes them to the pipeline, requiring a re-entry cycle to fix content the pipeline didn't introduce.\\n\\nThis is distinct from ANTI-002 (writer-introduced dash errors): the writer did not create these violations, and no amount of writer training or anti-pattern awareness prevents them.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\n- DOC-3167 GAP-002: Pre-existing Unicode en dashes (U+2013) found in files the pipeline was editing. T-003 converted 3 en dashes, T-006 converted 7 en dashes to double-hyphens. Hex-level grep confirmed zero remained after fix.\\n- ANTI-002 in the knowledge brief warned about writer-introduced dash issues, but this was a different failure mode (pre-existing content, not writer behavior).\\n- Both re-entry fixes passed first-attempt (style-only review).\\n\\n## Recommendation\\n\\nAdd a pre-submission encoding validation step: after the content writer produces output, run a hex-level grep for common encoding violations (U+2013 en dash, U+2014 em dash, U+201C/U+201D curly quotes, U+00A0 non-breaking space) across ALL lines of modified files, not just the diff. This catches pre-existing violations before gap hunting and eliminates one category of re-entry cycles.\\n\\n## Applicability\\n\\nApplicable to any pipeline run that modifies existing documentation files. The risk scales with the age and number of contributors to the file — older files with many contributors are more likely to contain encoding inconsistencies. Less applicable when creating entirely new files.\\n\"}"
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "toolu_vrtx_019mLqqjRU3uhJvtHtc8jxgd",
            "type": "function",
            "function": {
              "name": "create",
              "arguments": "{\"path\": \"/workspace/.docwriter/meta/domain-insights/DOM-023.md\", \"file_text\": \"---\\nid: DOM-023\\ntitle: \\\"Operations with non-standard permission checking patterns require dedicated documentation callouts\\\"\\ntype: domain-insight\\nconfidence: low\\ndomains: [\\\"content-security\\\", \\\"xperience-platform\\\", \\\"reference\\\"]\\ndiscoveredDate: \\\"2026-03-18T22:24:04Z\\\"\\nlastReferencedDate: \\\"2026-03-18T22:24:04Z\\\"\\nusageCount: 1\\nsourceTask: DOC-3167\\ninvariantsReferenced: []\\ndeprecated: false\\n---\\n\\n## Insight\\n\\nOperations that deviate from the standard permission checking pattern (e.g., READ + specific permission on the target page) require dedicated documentation callouts explaining the scope of the permission check. Three deviation patterns were identified in the Xperience page permission model:\\n\\n1. **Ancestor-chain checks**: Synchronize checks READ + SYNCHRONIZE on the page AND all ancestors in the content tree. A single missing permission on any ancestor blocks the operation.\\n2. **Subtree evaluation**: Delete-with-children checks DELETE on all child pages with broken ACL inheritance (evaluateChildPages=true), not just the target page.\\n3. **Parent-page checks**: Create and Clone check permissions on the PARENT page, not the page being created/cloned.\\n\\nWithout dedicated callouts, users assume the standard \\\"permission on this page\\\" model applies to all operations, leading to permission configuration errors.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\n- DOC-3167 GAP-003: Synchronize ancestor-chain check was undocumented. Code analysis had documented this behavior (permissionSummary), but the task planner omitted synchronize from scope. Gap hunter caught it in cycle 1.\\n- DOC-3167 T-005: Delete subtree check was in risk register and documented correctly on first attempt, showing that when non-standard checks ARE in scope, they can be documented successfully.\\n- DOC-3167 T-004: Clone permission (READ on source + CREATE on target parent) was documented as part of GAP-001 resolution.\\n\\n## Applicability\\n\\nApplicable to any permission documentation task where code analysis reveals operations with recursive, ancestor-chain, subtree, or parent-context permission checks. The task planner should flag these operations as requiring dedicated callout sections, and the content writer should explicitly describe the scope of the permission check (which entities are checked, in what order, and what happens when one check fails).\\n\"}"
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "toolu_vrtx_01Qr4EvSReWoFPuuGL1z25MJ",
            "type": "function",
            "function": {
              "name": "create",
              "arguments": "{\"path\": \"/workspace/.docwriter/meta/domain-insights/DOM-024.md\", \"file_text\": \"---\\nid: DOM-024\\ntitle: \\\"Workflow step role checks may be asymmetric across operations sharing the same ACL permission\\\"\\ntype: domain-insight\\nconfidence: low\\ndomains: [\\\"content-security\\\", \\\"xperience-platform\\\", \\\"reference\\\"]\\ndiscoveredDate: \\\"2026-03-18T22:24:04Z\\\"\\nlastReferencedDate: \\\"2026-03-18T22:24:04Z\\\"\\nusageCount: 1\\nsourceTask: DOC-3167\\ninvariantsReferenced: []\\ndeprecated: false\\n---\\n\\n## Insight\\n\\nOperations that share the same base ACL permission may have asymmetric additional checks at other layers. In the Xperience page permission model, both publish and unpublish require READ + UPDATE at the ACL layer, but publish additionally requires a workflow step role check (CanWorkWithContentItemInCurrentStep) while unpublish does NOT. This asymmetry is invisible when documenting permissions at the ACL layer alone — it only becomes apparent when examining the workflow layer independently.\\n\\nWhen documenting operations that share base permissions, each operation must be independently verified for additional checks at every layer. Grouping operations by shared ACL permission and assuming uniform behavior leads to documentation errors.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\n- DOC-3167 T-002: Risk register explicitly flagged the publish/unpublish asymmetry: \\\"the unpublish/revert distinction — workflow step check vs. no check — could be mischaracterized.\\\"\\n- DOC-3167 T-003: Workflows page (high technical accuracy risk) required careful per-operation documentation to avoid conflating publish and unpublish behavior despite their shared ACL permission.\\n- Strength: low (1 occurrence, single product-specific asymmetry)\\n\\n## Applicability\\n\\nApplicable to any permission documentation where multiple operations share a base permission but have different additional requirements at other layers (workflow, approval chain, feature flag). The task planner should instruct the content writer to document each operation independently, even when they appear grouped by shared permissions in code analysis output. Less applicable to simple permission models without layered checks.\\n\"}"
            }
          }
        ],
        "parsed": null
      }
    }
  ],
  "created": 1773872824,
  "model": "claude-opus-4.6",
  "object": "chat.completion"
}
